# Findings accepted deliberately.
#
# Every entry needs three things: the id, why it is accepted, and a date to look
# at it again. An ignore without a reason is a finding somebody forgot, not one
# anybody decided about — and it will still be here in a year.
#
# Prefer fixing over ignoring. Trivy's first run on this repository found eleven
# things; one of them was real and was fixed rather than listed here (the VPC
# endpoint security group allowed egress to 0.0.0.0/0 for "return traffic",
# which stateful security groups do not need — it is now confined to the VPC).
# The rest are below, because they are the design rather than defects in it.

# --- The load balancer is meant to be public ------------------------------------
# AWS-0053: "Load balancer is exposed publicly."
# The deliverable is a public, internet-facing API. An internal-only load
# balancer would not serve it. HTTPS only, HTTP redirects, and everything behind
# it sits in private subnets with no route in except through the listener.
# Review: 2027-02-01
AWS-0053

# --- Public subnets are meant to assign public IPs -------------------------------
# AWS-0164: "Subnet associates public IP address."
# This is what makes a public subnet public. Only the NAT gateways and the load
# balancer live there. Every task and the database are in private or database
# subnets; the database tier has no route to a NAT gateway at all.
# Review: 2027-02-01
AWS-0164

# --- Task egress is open, and needs to be ----------------------------------------
# AWS-0104: "Security group rule allows unrestricted egress to any IP address."
# The remaining instance is the ECS task security group. Tasks pull images from
# ECR, download the embedding model on first start, and write to CloudWatch.
# In prod the interface VPC endpoints keep most of that off the public internet,
# but the model download is a genuine outbound call to the internet.
#
# Closing this properly means an egress rule per destination prefix list, which
# is worth doing when the image set stops changing. It is a real hardening item,
# not a false positive — it is listed here so it stays visible.
# Review: 2027-02-01
AWS-0104

# --- Dev image tags are mutable on purpose ---------------------------------------
# AWS-0031: "Repository tags are mutable."
# `image_tag_mutability` is a variable: MUTABLE in dev so a tag can be
# overwritten on every push, IMMUTABLE in prod so nobody has to ask which code is
# running. The finding fires against the dev environment, where it is the
# intended setting.
# Review: 2027-02-01
AWS-0031
